            <h2>XML sitemap & SEO notes</h2>
            <p class="muted">For search engines, maintain a canonical <code>/sitemap.xml</code> and split large sitemaps into logical files (products, categories, static pages) if you exceed 50,000 URLs. Example recommended structure:</p>
            <ul class="value-list muted">
              <li>/sitemap-index.xml — references child sitemaps</li>
              <li>/sitemap-products.xml — product detail pages (changefreq: weekly)</li>
              <li>/sitemap-categories.xml — category pages (changefreq: daily)</li>
              <li>/sitemap-pages.xml — static and legal pages (changefreq: monthly)</li>
            </ul>

            <p class="muted note">Submit your sitemap to Google Search Console and Bing Webmaster Tools after each major site re-structure or product import. Include <code>&lt;lastmod&gt;</code> timestamps in ISO 8601 and correct <code>&lt;priority&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;changefreq&gt;</code> values.</p>

            <a class="xml-link" href="/sitemap.xml" aria-label="Download XML sitemap">Download XML sitemap (sitemap.xml)</a>

            <hr style="border:none;border-top:1px solid #eef2f7;margin:18px 0">

            <h2>How to use this page</h2>
            <ol class="value-list muted">
              <li>Developers: use it to verify routing and canonical URLs.</li>
              <li>SEO: reference changefreq/priority before generating XML sitemaps.</li>
              <li>Support &amp; CX: link to this page from internal docs to help agents locate pages quickly.</li>
            </ol>

            <p class="note">If you'd like, we can also generate a machine-readable sitemap (split into multiple files), add &lt;lastmod&gt; timestamps dynamically from your CMS, or create a small admin page to preview sitemap coverage.</p>

